{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>לֹא תִּרְצָח, לֹא תִּנְאָף, וגו׳.</b> After He completed the five utterances between man and the Omnipresent, blessed be He, He set opposite them the five utterances between man and his fellow, in the manner that they were recorded in the Mekhilta — how they would parallel this one opposite that one, along with those analogies. For what they said, that 'לֹא תִּרְצָח' corresponds to 'אָנֹכִי' — because whoever sheds blood, it is as if he diminishes the image and the likeness, as it is stated (Genesis 9:6): ״שֹׁפֵךְ דַּם הָאָדָם בָּאָדָם דָּמוֹ יִשָּׁפֵךְ, כִּי בְּצֶלֶם אֱלֹהִים עָשָׂה אֶת הָאָדָם״. And there is no need for this reasoning except to say [that even in the case of] one who says to another 'Kill me, please, kill me,' and he [the victim] forgives him the killing — even so, his blood shall be shed, for it is not given to forgiveness, because there is in him a divine portion from above, and a portion on high — who may release [it]? And this is the reason also for one who kills himself." "And the matter of the image and the likeness is this: in all His appearances, may He be blessed, His way is to appear in the likeness of the image of man, even though without doubt there is no image or likeness there, as it were, as it is written (Isaiah 40:25): ״וְאֶל מִי תְדַמְיוּנִי וְאֶשְׁוֶה יֹאמַר קָדוֹשׁ״, and it is written: ״מַה דְּמוּת תַּעַרְכוּ לוֹ״ (Isaiah 40:18). Nevertheless, when He appears, He shows the likeness of a man to the creatures, and on account of the preeminence of man He appears in this likeness, as it is written (Ezekiel 1:26): ״וְעַל הַכִּסֵּא דְּמוּת כְּמַרְאֵה אָדָם״. You see that in truth there was no appearance of a man there, but rather 'a likeness, as the appearance of a man.' And so He appeared upon the sea as a mighty warrior, and at Mount Sinai as an elder. And therefore it is fitting to conduct oneself with honor toward that likeness, and the murderer diminishes the likeness, for he causes that the Holy One, blessed be He, no longer wishes to appear in this likeness, since it is held in contempt among the creatures. And the murdered man who is seen is analogous to ״כִּי קִלְלַת אֱלֹהִים תָּלוּי״ (Deuteronomy 21:23), and there is no need to elaborate further on this." "And it appears to me to say that this is why the utterance 'לֹא תִּרְצָח' comes opposite 'אָנֹכִי' — because Jacob and Esau made a division between them: Jacob chose for himself YH as his portion, and HaShem as the allotment of his inheritance, while Esau chose for himself the craft of 'עַל חַרְבְּךָ תִחְיֶה' (Genesis 27:40). And therefore he denied the fundamental principle, as is derived (Bava Batra 16a) that he denied the fundamental principle, from the fact that it is written ״לָמָּה זֶּה לִי״ (Genesis 25:32), and it is written ״זֶה אֵלִי״ וגו׳ (Exodus 15:2). And therefore the commandment of 'אָנֹכִי' came to the house of Jacob, and corresponding to this He warned them not to hold to the craft of Esau, for that craft brought him to heresy. And for this reason, because of 'לֹא תִּרְצָח,' the children of Esau did not accept the Torah — for murder brings one to heresy regarding 'אָנֹכִי יְהוָה אֱלֹהֶיךָ,' and if so, the entire Torah in its entirety is nullified: if there is no Commander, there is no place for any commandment whatsoever. And therefore he [Esau] did not wish to accept even the rest of the utterances, for the matter of murder contradicts everything, for the murderer thinks that he is carrying out the will of the planet Mars, through the power of the celestial prince above, and thereby he denies His divinity, may He be blessed. And these are ancient teachings." "<b>לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים — כְּנֶגְדּוֹ לֹא תִּנְאָף.</b> As it is written concerning idolaters: ״הָאִשָּׁה הַמְנָאָפֶת״ וגו׳ (Ezekiel 16:32), and so [there are] many [such verses]. And it appears to me to say that adultery also causes one to worship idols, and the incident of Baal Peor at Shittim proves it (Numbers 25), and as it is written concerning intermarriage with the seven nations: ״כִּי יָסִיר אֶת בִּנְךָ מֵאַחֲרַי״ (Deuteronomy 7:4) — for there is nothing that brings to idolatry like the seduction of a strange woman, who with the portion of her lips casts him away from HaShem. And this matter is well-known and widely publicized, and widely publicized things require no proof." "<b>לֹא תִשָּׂא — כְּנֶגֶד לֹא תִּגְנֹב.</b> For one who steals will end up swearing falsely, as it is stated (Leviticus 19:11–12): ״לֹא תִּגְנֹבוּ״ וגו׳ ״וְלֹא תִשָּׁבְעוּ״ וגו׳ — for this one drags along that one. And therefore it is stated (Zechariah 5:4): ״וּבָאָה אֶל בֵּית הַגַּנָּב וְאֶל בֵּית הַנִּשְׁבָּע בִּשְׁמִי לַשցָּקֶר וְכִלַּתּוּ״ וגו׳. It equates these two transgressions, because one drags along the other and their underlying matter is one. For the one who steals acts as though the Eye above does not see, as it were; and likewise this one who swears falsely in order to verify his lie before people — he too acts as though the Eye above does not see, as it were. And therefore one who commits this [theft] falls into that [false oath], even if he swore about some other matter that is unrelated to theft. Thus it appears to me, in addition to what is recorded in the Mekhilta." "<b>Remember the Sabbath day — over against it, you shall not testify falsely.</b> For every desecrator of the Sabbath testifies that the Holy One, blessed be He, did not create His world in six days and rest on the seventh. And it appears to me, since it is said concerning the Sabbath, ״מִמְּצוֹא חֶפְצְךָ וְדַבֵּר דָּבָר״ (Isaiah 58:13), and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shabbat 113a) that your speech on the Sabbath should not be like your weekday speech — and the reason for this matter is that all of a person's actions are performed with his tools of labor, but the action of the Holy One, blessed be He, is through speech alone, for ״בִּדְבַר ה׳ שָׁמַיִם נַעֲשׂוּ״ and ״הוּא אָמַר וַיֶּהִי״ (Psalms 33:6–9). Accordingly, when a person rests on the Sabbath and does no labor with his tools of labor, this rest does not resemble the rest of the Holy One, blessed be He, for the Holy One, blessed be He, rested even from the speech of His mouth, whereas the person rests only from the activity of his tools of labor. Therefore it is said, ״וְדַבֵּר דָּבָר״ — that your speech on the Sabbath should not be like your weekday speech, in order to remember the rest of the Holy One, blessed be He, who rested from speech. And from Him you shall see and so shall you do — not to speak on the Sabbath of your own affairs but only of the affairs of Heaven. And through remembering the Sabbath day — for he is obligated to sanctify the utterance of his lips not to speak weekday words — then at least on the other days of the week, even though a vessel used for sacred purposes on the Sabbath may be used for weekday purposes during the days of the week, nevertheless he will at least sanctify the words of his lips not to let falsehood issue from his mouth and not to testify falsely against his fellow." "<b>Honor your father, etc. — over against it, you shall not covet.</b> The Mekhilta concludes that everyone who covets ultimately begets a son who curses him, and this matter requires explanation, for what has this to do with that? And it appears to explain, since everyone who covets his fellow's wife certainly fantasizes about her at the time of relations, and it is as though the son were born to him from another woman whom he coveted and fantasized about. Therefore he does not honor his mother as is fitting, because his father was fantasizing about another woman. And he will not honor his father as is fitting either, for one who covets women has no intention to beget children but only to satisfy his desire, and he did not intend toward this son to beget him; therefore it is as though he is not his son, and therefore he will not honor him either, for everything follows the intention. And the coveting of money is also tied to the honoring of father and mother, for one who covets others' money does not honor them as is fitting because he is inflamed by the pyre of money-lust — for honor requires expenditure of one's wealth to feed them, give them drink, and clothe them, and this coveter, without doubt, has a grudging eye even toward his father and mother, lest he give them enough for their needs."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:18.367193209Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}